  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 240 PM Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Seasonable temperatures, dry weather and an \nincrease in cloudiness are expected this weekend. \n\nIncreasing SW flow aloft ahead of a pair of mid and upper level \ndisturbances (one currently over the lower MS Valley and another \nover the Southern Plains) is resulting in broken to overcast mid and \nhigh clouds overspreading central NC from the south and west this \nafternoon. This will continue through tomorrow, but no rain is \nexpected given a dry and stable air mass in the lower levels.\n\nHigh temperatures this weekend will be near normal, generally mid-to-\nupper-60s today and a couple degrees warmer than that tomorrow. \nTemperatures for trick-or-treating this evening will be mostly in \nthe mid-50s to mid-60s. While low-level thicknesses will be lower \ntonight, the effect will be somewhat offset by the increased cloud \ncover, so bumped up forecast lows on Sunday morning a tad to mid-40s \nto 50. Monday morning's lows should again be similar, perhaps a \ndegree or two warmer.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Forecast confidence is high in unseasonably mild \ntemperatures next week but low regarding amounts with the next \nchance of rain late in the week (favored Thu-Fri). \n\nThere remains considerable model spread and forecast uncertainty \nregarding episodes of stream interaction and phasing of the flow \nupstream of a mid-level ridge that will otherwise remain and \nredevelop just offshore the srn Middle Atlantic coast for most of \nnext week. There is good model consensus that the ridge will weaken \nand drift to near and east of Bermuda by the end of the week, which \nshould allow for perturbed, cyclonic flow to progress ewd and across \ncntl NC by Fri. Although the geometry of the cyclonic flow is less \ncertain, there remains a consistent signal in model guidance for the \nstronger height falls to \"lift\" and pass to the north and west of \ncntl NC at least through Sat. \n\nAt the surface, the most dominant and highest predictability feature \nwill be a ridge, related a 1034 mb Canadian high now centered over \nthe lwr Great Lakes, which will extend across the Middle Atlantic \nthrough the middle of next week. Similar uncertainty regarding the \nevolution of the trough aloft noted above exists with the strength \nand timing of passage of the accompanying surface frontal zone, \nprobably through cntl NC on or around Friday based on recent model \nguidance. While the probability of rain will increase ahead of that \nfront by Thu, then maximize with its passage, rainfall amounts \nremain highly uncertain; and the prospects for a soaking rain area-\nwide are in doubt.",
